.SH NAME
hed\-whichkey \- When you start a multi\-key chord and pause, whichkey
lists the candidate completions in the message bar \[em] a small
read\-only popup that goes away the moment you finish the sequence or
wait long enough for the dispatcher to time out.
.PP
When you start a multi\-key chord and pause, whichkey lists the
candidate completions in the message bar \[em] a small read\-only popup
that goes away the moment you finish the sequence or wait long enough
for the dispatcher to time out.
.PP
It\[cq]s pure discoverability: type \f[CR]<space>\f[R] and you can see
every leader binding without leaving the editor.
.PP
When the current buffer has a filetype with filetype\-specific bindings
(e.g.\ \f[CR]dired\f[R], \f[CR]mail\f[R], \f[CR]mail\-message\f[R]),
they appear in an extra \f[CR]── Filetype (<ft>) ──\f[R] section below
the prefix\-match list \[em] so the keys a \f[CR]mail\-message\f[R]
exposes (\f[CR]r\f[R], \f[CR]a\f[R], \f[CR]A\f[R], \f[CR]f\f[R],
\f[CR]q\f[R], \&...)
are discoverable even though they don\[cq]t share the active prefix.
.SH What you see
.IP
.EX
<space>
 ff         fzf                | fr         recent files     | fc         command picker
 ws         split              | wv         vsplit            | ww         focus next
 sd         rg                 | sa         rgword            | ss         ssearch
 ts         send paragraph     | tt         tmux toggle       | tH         whichkey toggle
 ...
.EE
.IP \[bu] 2
The first line shows the active sequence (and any numeric prefix \[em]
e.g.\ \f[CR]42 d\f[R] while typing \f[CR]42d\&...\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
Each cell shows the \f[B]tail\f[R] (what more you\[cq]d type) plus the
binding\[cq]s description.
.IP \[bu] 2
Entries are sorted alphabetically by tail.
ASCII puts \f[CR]\[aq] \[aq]\f[R] first, so any nested\-leader binding
floats to the top.
.IP \[bu] 2
1\[en]4 columns based on terminal width (\f[CR]MIN_COL_W = 28\f[R],
\f[CR]\[dq] | \[dq]\f[R] separators, picks the largest N that fits).
.IP \[bu] 2
Capped at 18 rows; overflow is marked \f[CR]...\f[R].
.SH How it works
Driven entirely by two hooks added in v1.3.0:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]HOOK_KEYBIND_FEED\f[R] fires after every keypress that goes
through the dispatcher.
The plugin reads the snapshot (active sequence, pending count, full
match list) and renders to the message bar when
\f[CR]partial == true\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]HOOK_KEYBIND_INVOKE\f[R] fires just before a binding\[cq]s
callback runs.
The plugin clears its display so the popup disappears the instant a
sequence resolves.
.PP
No polling, no extra render plumbing, no timers \[em] the message bar
already auto\-grows for newlines.
.PP
The dispatcher\[cq]s sequence timeout (\f[CR]SEQUENCE_TIMEOUT_MS\f[R] in
\f[CR]src/keybinds.c\f[R], default 5 s) decides how long whichkey stays
up if you walk away mid\-chord.
.SH Toggle
.IP
.EX
:whichkey [on|off|toggle]
.EE
.PP
Toggling \f[CR]off\f[R] calls \f[CR]hook_unregister\f[R] for both hooks,
so the dispatcher does literally zero work for whichkey while it\[cq]s
disabled.
Toggling back on re\-subscribes.
.PP
\f[CR]<space>th\f[R] is bound to \f[CR]:whichkey toggle\f[R] by default.
